  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Multiple rounds of heavy thunderstorms will affect \nthe area through today into tonight bringing the threat of both\nsevere weather and flash flooding. Additional thunderstorms are\npossible on Friday.\n\nThere are still some showers and storms ongoing at this hour but\nthese have diminished in intensity compared to earlier this\nmorning. By later in the morning through a good portion of the \nafternoon there looks to be a lull in storms as some sunshine \ndevelops with it getting quite warm and humid as the area breaks\ninto the warm sector with a WSE flow. This will lead to further\ndestabilization to occur. In fact ML CAPES looks to max out in \nthe 2000-3000 j/kg range. This will set the stage for additional\nstorms to occur along a cold front working south mainly as we \nget into Thursday evening. There's some uncertainty on the \ntiming and coverage of these storms and at this point the Storm \nPrediction Center has most of the region in a MARGINAL risk of \nsevere thunderstorms meaning isolated severe storms are \npossible. Only portions of our eastern MD shore counties are \ngrazed by the SLIGHT risk area that's focused mainly to our \nsouth and west. Similar to this morning, PWATs will be quite \nhigh so flash flooding will be a threat as well. \n\nFriday's SPC outlook only has a small portion of the Maryland \nEastern Shore and southern Delaware in a Marginal risk for severe \nweather. Th rest of the area includes general thunderstorms (no \nsevere risk), though there is still some uncertainty with this. Our \nlatest forecast has about a 40-70% chance of showers and \nthunderstorms from about I-78 on southwards, including the entire \nPhiladelphia metro area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Following the brief cool down that occurred \nWednesday, hot and humid conditions are once again expected across \nthe region on Thursday as the frontal boundary lifts north again. \nWith the front to the north, high temperatures are forecast to climb \ninto the upper 80s to mid 90s for many areas (low to mid 80s far \nnorth). These temperatures combined with dewpoints in the low to mid \n70s will again result in heat indices well into the 90s to low 100s \nsouth of I-78, perhaps even as high as 105 degrees for Delmarva.\n\nA Heat Advisory remains in effect for portions of southeastern PA, \nNJ, and Delmarva. North of the current advisory area, maximum heat \nindices are forecast to generally top out in the low to mid 90s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...The Labor Day Weekend will feature mostly dry \nweather with at or below normal temperatures and lower humidity.\n\nBy Saturday, current model consensus indicates the frontal boundary \nwill be pushing well south of our region, with Canadian high \npressure building across the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic. This will \nlargely end any shower and thunderstorm potential. Thus, Labor Day \nWeekend is still shaping up to feature nice weather, with \ntemperatures trending at or slightly below normal and lower \nhumidity.",
